                  snippet:
                    "Some studies report considerable improvements in employee performance and satisfaction (Fathima and Suresh Kumar, 2025) while others emphasise contextual factors such as organisational culture, digital capability and employee adaptability (Maity ...\nSome studies report considerable improvements in employee performance and satisfaction (Fathima and Suresh Kumar, 2025) while others emphasise contextual factors such as organisational culture, digital capability and employee adaptability (Maity and Lee, 2025). These inconsistencies suggest that more underlying behavioural and technological processes define productivity outcomes rather than remote work policies.\nAlthough scholarly interest has increased, there has been little research that integrates technology acceptance, task-technology fit, and knowledge-based perspectives into a single framework to explain how technology-driven behavioural intentions can improve productivity in remote work. Most previous papers investigate organisational-level adoption outcomes (Ferdous et al., 2025) or the impact of digital intention on employee satisfaction and performance (Sansovini, 2025), leaving a gap in the literature on how to directly link digital intention mechanisms to quantifiable productivity outcomes.\ntechnological barriers, communication breakdowns, and difficulties in maintaining work-life balance. Moreover, concerns have been raised about the potential impact of remote work on team cohesion, organizational culture, and ... Against this backdrop, it is essential to empirically examine the impact of remote work on employee productivity · and satisfaction to inform organizational policies and practices.\nEvaluate Job Satisfaction and Well-being: Assess the effects of remote work on employee job satisfaction, psychological well-being, and work-life balance. This includes examining factors such as job autonomy, social · support, stress levels, and perceived quality of work-life integration. ... practices in shaping the experiences of [17]remote employees. This includes analyzing the effectiveness of remote · work policies, communication strategies, and technology infrastructure in supporting remote work",
